 "area": "retina",
 "description": "DEMOTION HIERARCHY (block 198000) on top of 196000's staleness_decay atom. A held trace carries integer freshness = Ashr(C0, IDiv(dEpoch, half_life)); freshness-thresholded TIERS (FRESH>=t_fresh / GIST>=t_gist / LATENT) drive a DEMOTE atom (dh_tier + dh_demote) that COARSENS the stored representation as freshness decays WITHOUT refresh: FRESH keeps full detail, GIST sheds it to Ashr(detail,k_gist) (an integer bit-plane LOD summary), LATENT keeps only a presence marker (stored=0, present=1). THE DEMOTE ATOM ONLY EVER DEMOTES -- freshness->0 leaves a LATENT trace, NEVER an empty cell; it contains no del_node. dh_promote resets the innovation clock on a new observation so the trace climbs back to FRESH and RECOVERS full detail from the incoming data (reversible recall / DRAM-refresh). Deletion is a SEPARATE, high-order (9), bottom-tier-gated faculty (dh_delete_latent) behind its own delete_enabled flag -- the ONLY rule that deletes, never wired into decay. Integer-native (Ashr / Shl / IDiv / IfThenElse / Gte / Eq); only the cross-frame recon uses a shift. OFF by default: rules need a HeldTierParams node the adapter creates only on opt-in; absent it every existing path is byte-identical. NO Rust, NO new Term.",
 "microtheory": {
  "name": "demotion_hierarchy",
  "description": "Decay DEMOTES, it does not DELETE. Freshness-thresholded tiers coarsen a trace's representation (registers->L1->disk / episodic->semantic gist / cache eviction); re-observation promotes it back; deletion is a separate bottom-tier faculty."
 },
